  "// archetypes": "Transformer projection shapes, where nearly all production GEMM time goes. M carries tokens and moves with batch and sequence length; N and K are model widths and take a few architecture-fixed values, which is why they are perturbed by whole 128-element multiples rather than scaled.",
  "// mixture": "Same shares as conv_fwd; see the note there.",
  "// flops": "2*M*N*K -- one multiply and one add per output element per K step. A property of the problem, not of the kernel: the same count however it is tiled. tflops = flops / measured time.",
  "flops": {
    "*": [
      2,
      "$q.M",
      "$q.N",
      "$q.K"
    ]
  },
  "// elements": "A (M*K) + B (K*N) + C (M*N). Element count across every tensor the operation touches, inputs and outputs. Dtype-free on purpose: bytes is elements times the dtype width, and the width table belongs to the tool rather than repeated as a nested dtype lookup in seven declarations. gbs = bytes / measured time, which is the metric that ranks a memory-bound kernel where tflops cannot.",
  "elements": {
    "+": [
      {
        "*": [
          "$q.M",
          "$q.K"
        ]
      },
      {
        "*": [
          "$q.K",
          "$q.N"
        ]
      },
      {
        "*": [
          "$q.M",
          "$q.N"
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
